# Billing setup

The generated Stripe integration persists an opaque billing subject for the
session-authorized owner, customer provisioning intent, an immutable checkout
attempt, its session ID, and verified event receipts. Email is contact data only.
Run the generated migrations before mounting billing routes.

Configure `BILLING_PROVIDER=stripe`, `BILLING_ACCOUNT_ID=acct_...`,
`BILLING_API_KEY`, `BILLING_WEBHOOK_SECRET`, `BILLING_REDIRECT_URL` (HTTPS), and
`BILLING_ALLOWED_PLAN_IDS` (server-owned recurring price IDs). Test keys use a
separate namespace from live keys. API credentials must have permission to read
the account, customers, Checkout Sessions and subscriptions, and create customers
and Checkout Sessions. The generated integration uses platform accounts, not
Stripe Connect impersonation. Configure the webhook endpoint with Stripe API
version `2025-03-31.basil` (see the capital adapter's pinned version).

Live keys alone do not activate real charges. After reviewing the deployed
offer, merchant settings, webhook, reconciliation and sandbox evidence, set
`BILLING_LIVE_ACKNOWLEDGEMENT=I_UNDERSTAND_REAL_CHARGES`. Without that exact
value the generated live path remains unavailable. Test credentials do not
require this acknowledgement. This operational switch does not certify the
merchant's legal obligations or the deployment's readiness.

Before enabling `/billing/portal`, save an active default customer-portal
configuration in the Stripe account for the selected test/live mode. Review its
allowed plan changes, cancellation and payment-method settings; API credentials
also need permission to create portal sessions. The generated route authorizes
the persisted customer ID and uses the account's default configuration, as
described in the [Stripe session API](https://docs.stripe.com/api/customer_portal/sessions/create).
A working Checkout does not establish that the portal is configured. Test its
authenticated handoff separately before offering subscription management.

Subscribe to `checkout.session.completed`, `checkout.session.expired`,
`checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded`, `checkout.session.async_payment_failed`,
`customer.subscription.created`, `customer.subscription.updated`,
`customer.subscription.deleted`, `customer.subscription.paused` and
`customer.subscription.resumed`. Mount the exact
POST webhook route behind `verify_billing_webhook`; retain the framework's WAF,
secure headers and exact signed-webhook CSRF exemption. Live processing uses
signature verification followed by atomic event completion; do not add a separate
replay-claim middleware before it. Unknown event kinds are rejected, so configure
only supported notifications.

Checkout is an authenticated CSRF-protected POST. Keep the price allowlist and
pricing page in sync. Add the exact `https://checkout.stripe.com` origin to the
pricing page's CSP `form-action`; retain server validation and browser testing.
Session creation/navigation never grants an entitlement. Signed events trigger
current provider-state reads. Revision fencing prevents delayed reads from
replacing a newer committed state, and receipt/subscription writes commit together.
The application's access policy must use reconciled subscription status and period;
`active` is subscription state, not proof of invoice settlement. Require separate
invoice evidence for application features that specifically depend on settlement.

## Plan-gated billing report (v13)

The SaaS blueprint mounts authenticated `GET /reports/billing`. `make:billing`
also generates `billing_controller::billing_report`; applications mounting it
must supply their existing authenticated `BillingIdentity`, CSRF/WAF and secure
headers. Configure `BILLING_REPORT_PLAN_IDS` as a nonempty, duplicate-free subset
of `BILLING_ALLOWED_PLAN_IDS`. Missing/invalid configuration returns 503.

Each request loads the durable owner binding, establishes a revision fence,
reads the account/subscription from Stripe, and commits the reconciliation before
checking `rullst-capital::entitlements`. Only `active` subscriptions in an exact
allowed plan with a future period end qualify. Trials, past-due/canceled state,
missing state and expired periods deny access. Reads older than 30 seconds deny
access; the request has a 20-second deadline and never falls back to stale state.
Expect provider latency and read costs; apply authenticated rate limits for the
deployment's budget. Responses contain the current plan/status/period, use
`no-store` and expose no provider customer or subscription identifiers.

`RULLST_ENV=production` requires live evidence and the existing real-charge
acknowledgement. Development uses Stripe sandbox evidence; offline credentials
remain deterministic denial, even if editable CMS subscription rows say active.
The scope is the configured Stripe account/mode within this application's
database, and the owner comes from the authenticated session. This starter does
not resolve arbitrary tenant headers or add shared workspace membership.
Multi-tenant applications must supply their own authoritative scoped adapter.

Authorization takes effect at the read/commit boundary; a later cancellation
cannot retract an already generated response. Sensitive writes need a domain
transaction or another explicit concurrency strategy. Updating a dependency
does not replace existing generated modules. Review the new report module and
route before installing them in an existing application; no new billing schema
is required. The independent profile/rights export is never gated by a paid plan.

Retry a failed checkout POST to resume the same persisted attempt. An existing
open session is retrieved and reused. Provider mutation retries reuse the exact
key for at most 23 hours; older unknown outcomes are recovered by read-only
customer metadata search or bounded customer/session listing. No match, ambiguous
results or exhausted listing bounds return 503 without creating a duplicate.
Customer search can lag and is unavailable to merchants in India. Retry later
or locate the original customer in the Stripe dashboard. An authorized server
operator can call `billing_controller::recover_stripe_customer` with the local
identity and known customer ID; it verifies account, mode and the original opaque
metadata before binding it. This recovery function is not exposed as a route.
Retain the database across deploys. Back up `billing_state`, `billing_events` and
`subscriptions` together. Do not delete a pending intent to bypass reconciliation.
The inbox is retained durably; plan audited retention and reconciliation for your
application's data retention policy. An owner retains up to 64 previous
checkout bindings to prevent old notifications changing a replacement subscription.

HTTP 409 can indicate an existing subscription or an incompatible checkout attempt.
A checkout that has just expired is reconciled before the next POST creates a new
attempt. Failed/repeated requests do not create quota consumption; an application
adding billing quotas should count newly persisted session IDs, not HTTP requests.

Empty/mock credentials remain explicit offline demonstrations. The legacy email
fixture handler never processes live events. Other generated providers remain
fixtures until their durable application integration is implemented. Existing
applications must merge these generated modules and add the new billing tables in
a new migration; updating the dependency does not overwrite existing controllers.
